 "Past tokens' K and V never change (causal mask), so they can be stored.": "",
 "Example: after \"The cat sat on the\" the cache holds K,V for these {#} tokens in every layer; generating <0/> adds one more entry, giving {#}.": "",
 "Why only K and V — not Q? Queries are used once (for the current token) and then never needed again. Keys and values are needed by <0>every future token</0>.": "",
 "Every past token's <0>keys and values</0> are saved so decode never recomputes them: each new token only computes its own K, V and attends to the cache.": "",
 "Cost: <0>{#} KiB per token</0> in the reference model = {#} (K,V) × {#} layers × {#} KV heads × {#} × {#} bytes. It grows linearly with context × batch size.": "",
 "At long contexts and large batches the cache can exceed the {#} GB of weights — GQA, quantised KV and paging exist to tame this.": "",
 "GQA shares each K/V head among {#} query heads: {#} KV heads instead of {#} gives a {#}× smaller cache (<0>GQA cost</0>).": "",
 "Prompt caching in chat APIs reuses a stored prefix cache across requests (<0>cost & caching</0>).": "",
 "Without a cache, recomputing everything every step makes total work grow ~quadratically with output length.": "",
