 "A {#} GB model becomes ≈ {#} GB (INT8) or ≈ {#} GB (INT4) — the difference between needing a data-centre GPU and fitting on a laptop or phone-class device. Details on the next level.": "",
 "Related: the KV cache can be quantised too (<0>KV cache calculator</0>), and training uses BF16 for different reasons (<1>scaling &amp; precision</1>).": "",
 "Quantization stores weights with fewer bits: BF16 ({#} bit) → INT8 ({#} bit) → INT4 ({#} bit) cuts weight memory by {#}× or {#}×.": "",
 "Since decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, fewer bytes per weight usually also means faster decoding.": "",
 "The price is rounding error; careful schemes (per-group scales, outlier handling) keep quality loss small, but it is not zero.": "",
 "Symmetric INT8: <0/>, <1/>, dequantised weight = <2/>.": "",
 "One outlier weight inflates the scale and hurts all the others — hence per-group scales (e.g. {#} scale per {#}–{#} weights).": "",
 "Quality impact depends on model, method and task; INT8 is often near-lossless, INT4 shows some degradation (hedge: verify on your task).": "",
