 "<0><1/>total tokens/s</0>": "",
 "Step time = max(memory time, compute time): weights are streamed once per step no matter how many sequences share it; each extra sequence adds its own KV reads. Throughput climbs steeply, then flattens as the KV cache dominates (and it must also fit in GPU memory — see <0>KV cache</0>).": "",
 "<0>Illustrative values.</0> A100-class round numbers ({#} TB/s, {#} TFLOP/s), ideal kernels, no scheduling overhead.": "",
 "▼ Dive in: <0>Continuous</0>": "",
 "<0>Batching</0>: serve many requests per weight pass. Weights are read once for all sequences, so throughput (tokens/s overall) rises almost linearly until the GPU becomes compute-bound or KV memory runs out.": "",
 "<0>Continuous batching</0> swaps finished requests out and new ones in at every step, keeping the GPU full.": "",
 "<0>Speculative decoding</0>: a small draft model guesses k tokens; the big model checks all k in <1>one</1> pass and keeps the correct prefix — the output distribution is unchanged.": "",
 "Expected tokens per big-model pass = ({#} − α^(k+{#})) / ({#} − α), where α is the per-token acceptance rate (assumed independent).": "",
 "Batching improves throughput but each user's tokens/s may fall as the batch gets heavier; the KV cache bounds the batch size (<0>KV cache</0>).": "",
 "Hardware numbers are illustrative; speedups in real systems depend on draft quality and overheads.": "",
